@namespace NSail.Components

@* What a menu says rather than offers: who you are at its top, the version at its foot. It
   is not an item — no click, no tab stop — and it is presentational to the list it renders
   inside, so a menu never offers an option that does nothing. The rule that separates it from
   the items is drawn by position (ns-mud.css), not written here: a block that opens the menu
   is ruled underneath and one that closes it is ruled above, so neither end needs a divider
   the caller has to remember. *@
@* Escape from a control the block holds (a menu's own search) closes the menu: the vendor
   answers Escape on the list's wrapper only while the menu holds a row, and a menu that is a
   search alone holds none until something is typed. A control that answers Escape itself (a
   lookup's open list) stops it before it gets here; a menu with rows is closed by the vendor
   too, and a second close of a closed menu is absorbed. *@
<div class="ns-menu-block" role="presentation" @onkeydown="OnKeyDown">
    @ChildContent
</div>

@code {

    [CascadingParameter]
    MudMenu? Menu { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    Task OnKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs args)
    {
        if (Menu is null || args.Key != "Escape")
        {
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        return Menu.CloseMenuAsync();
    }
}
